       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="why-greenwich" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Why Greenwich Was Chosen">
    <h2>4. Why Greenwich Was Chosen</h2>
    <p>
      Selecting a single starting point for the world's time zones was the subject of intense debate. During the International Meridian Conference in 1884, several locations were proposed as candidates for the Prime Meridian. Some delegations argued that the zero meridian should be placed in a politically neutral location, such as the Bering Strait, Jerusalem, or the Azores. The French delegation strongly advocated for a meridian passing through Paris.
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite these proposals, Greenwich was selected as the Prime Meridian due to overwhelming practical and economic considerations:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing Shipping Charts</strong>: By 1884, approximately 72% of the world's commercial shipping maps and navigational charts already used Greenwich as their reference meridian. Selecting a different location would have forced naval authorities worldwide to discard and reprint their entire library of maritime maps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>US Railroad Standard</strong>: The United States had recently standardized its massive railway system using Greenwich as the reference line for its timezone offsets. Since the US was a rapidly growing economic power, this standardized railroad baseline carried significant weight.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Observational Equipment</strong>: The Royal Observatory possessed the Airy Transit Circle, a high-precision transit instrument built by Sir George Biddell Airy in 1850. This telescope was considered the most accurate instrument in the world for tracking the passage of stars across the meridian.
      </li>
    </ul>

