       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="how-gmt-works" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="How Greenwich Mean Time Works">
    <h2>5. How Greenwich Mean Time Works</h2>
    <p>
      To understand how GMT functions, we must distinguish between apparent solar time and mean solar time. **Apparent solar time** is measured directly by the position of the sun in the sky (as seen on a sundial). However, because the Earth's orbit around the sun is elliptical and the Earth's axis is tilted relative to the orbital plane, the speed of our planet varies throughout the year. This causes solar days to fluctuate in length.
    </p>
    <p>
      **Mean solar time** resolves this inconsistency by using a hypothetical "mean sun" that travels along the celestial equator at a perfectly constant speed. GMT is calculated based on this averaged mean solar day (exactly 24 hours). The difference between apparent solar time and mean solar time is calculated using the **Equation of Time**:
    </p>
    <div style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); margin: 1rem 0; font-family: monospace; text-align: center;">
      Equation of Time = Apparent Solar Time - Mean Solar Time
    </div>
    <p>
      This difference varies throughout the year, ranging from roughly -14 minutes in February to +16 minutes in November. By averaging these astronomical variations, GMT provides a stable 24-hour baseline.
    </p>

