       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="gmt-offset" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Understanding GMT Offsets">
    <h2>6. Understanding GMT Offsets</h2>
    <p>
      Time zones are defined as deviations from the Prime Meridian. Because the Earth rotates 360 degrees in 24 hours, each 15-degree longitudinal band corresponds to one hour of time. Moving East from Greenwich results in positive offsets (+), while moving West results in negative offsets (-). 
    </p>
    <p>
      The International Date Line (at 180° longitude) acts as the global transition point where the calendar date changes, effectively creating the boundary where -12 and +12 hour offsets meet.
    </p>

