    <h2>11. Real-World Coordination Examples</h2>
    <p>
      To clarify how these standards operate, let us examine common scenarios where timezone confusion occurs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Scheduling a Webinar</strong>: If you schedule a meeting for 3:00 PM GMT in November, colleagues in London will join at 3:00 PM local time. However, if you schedule the meeting for 3:00 PM GMT in June, London will be observing BST (British Summer Time, UTC+1), meaning they will join at 4:00 PM local time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Financial Market Openings</strong>: The London Stock Exchange (LSE) operates from 8:00 AM to 4:30 PM local time. During the winter, this matches GMT. During the summer, it matches BST (UTC+1), which shifts the absolute opening hour for traders in New York (EST/EDT) and Tokyo (JST).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Programming Code Snippets</strong>:
        <p>
          Developers use UTC/GMT representations to parse and format timestamps consistently across client browsers. Review this typical pattern:
        </p>
        <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`// Format current date in GMT/UTC ISO string
const date = new Date();
console.log(date.toUTCString()); // Output: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 08:24:57 GMT`}</pre>
      </li>
    </ul>
